-- The OTHER half of the refused band, and the fixture that keeps the
-- diagnostic honest.
--
-- `zero-arity.l4` beside this file shows an input with NO subject. This one has
-- a subject — `severity exceeds` takes an `ASSUME`d TYPE as its first parameter,
-- which is exactly what an author following the old wording of the error
-- message would have written — and it is refused anyway, because the real
-- condition is on ARITY: `classifyPred` (`L4.Blawx.Lower`) places an input as an
-- attribute only at `(category)` or `(category) -> value`, and Blawx's
-- relationship blocks do not start until total arity 3. A two-place input falls
-- in the gap between them.
--
-- The message must therefore name the arity band, not the first parameter. It
-- used to say "… whose first parameter is not a category … an ASSUME must take
-- the thing it is about … as its first parameter", which for THIS module is
-- advice the author has already followed — there is no edit that satisfies it.
-- Keeping the module as a fixture is what stops that wording coming back.
--
-- What a user does about it: give the second argument a category of its own and
-- add a third (a relationship), or fold the NUMBER into the decision that reads
-- it (`ASSUME `severity` c IS A NUMBER` and compare in the DECIDE, which is
-- what `alcohol.l4` and `antisocial.l4` do).

§ `Arity two with a category subject`

ASSUME Consequence IS A TYPE

GIVEN c IS A Consequence
ASSUME `is severe` c IS A BOOLEAN

GIVEN c IS A Consequence
      n IS A NUMBER
ASSUME `severity exceeds` c n IS A BOOLEAN

@export A consequence that is severe and over the numeric threshold.
GIVEN c IS A Consequence
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `qualifies` c IF
        `is severe` c
    AND `severity exceeds` c 10
